<%# Plausible, and whatever the last request wants counted. See app/services/analytics.rb
    for what is measured and what is refused, and concerns/tracked_events.rb for
    how an event gets from a controller to here.

    The stub on the second line is Plausible's own: it defines window.plausible
    synchronously so anything that fires before the async script has landed is
    queued and replayed rather than dropped. That is what lets lib/analytics.js
    send an event on turbo:load without knowing or caring whether the script is
    down yet.

    Turbo keeps this working across visits without any help. It copies script
    elements it has not seen before and leaves the ones it has, so the two tags
    below load exactly once per full page load and are not re-run on a Turbo
    visit — which is right, because a second plausible.init() would be a second
    set of history listeners and every pageview would be counted twice. %>
<%# Measured against whoever is actually at the keyboard, which is not always
    current_user: an admin viewing the app as a student is still the admin, and
    the "not for admins" rule exists precisely so the owner's own clicking
    about does not become most of the dashboard. Impersonation is the heaviest
    clicking about there is. %>
<% if Analytics.enabled?(impersonator || current_user) %>
  <script async src="<%= Analytics.script_url %>"></script>
  <script>
    window.plausible=window.plausible||function(){(plausible.q=plausible.q||[]).push(arguments)},plausible.init=plausible.init||function(i){plausible.o=i||{}};
    plausible.init({ transformRequest: <%= Analytics.path_normalizer.html_safe %> })
  </script>

  <%# Trace, under the same gate and with a stub of its own, so a trace.track()
      from lib/analytics.js before d.js has landed is queued rather than lost.
      Cookieless: it stores nothing on the visitor's device, so there is no
      consent bar, and the Trace server tells visits apart with a hash salted
      daily that it can't reverse (a site used by children in the EU is exactly
      where a stored id would have needed one). Turbo sees these two tags once
      per full load, like Plausible's, and the collector hooks pushState itself.
      Numbers in a path fold to :id in the collector; the token routes are
      named for it in Analytics::TRACE_ROUTES. %>
  <script>window.trace=window.trace||{q:[]};["track","identify","page","init","consent"].forEach(function(m){trace[m]=trace[m]||function(){trace.q.push([m,arguments])}})</script>
  <script src="<%= Analytics.trace_script_url %>" data-key="<%= Analytics.trace_key %>"
          data-routes="<%= Analytics::TRACE_ROUTES.join(", ") %>" defer></script>
<% end %>

<%# An admin signing in from a page that loaded the scripts as a stranger: Turbo
    keeps those scripts running, so this page tells them to stop instead. Trace
    is told "no" (it remembers that on this device, which is the point: the
    owner's own browsing never counts again) and Plausible is told to ignore
    this browser the way its docs describe. %>
<% if (impersonator || current_user)&.admin? %>
  <meta name="analytics-off" content="admin">
<% end %>

<%# Who is signed in, for Trace's DAU, MAU and retention: a keyed hash, never
    the id (Analytics.trace_identity). A meta tag for the same reason as the
    event below — Turbo swaps it on every visit, so signing in, signing out and
    switching into a child's profile all reach Trace on the next page, which
    lib/analytics.js reads on turbo:load. Under the same switch as the script:
    an admin's sessions, impersonated or not, are never counted. %>
<% if Analytics.enabled?(impersonator || current_user) && (identity = Analytics.trace_identity(current_user)) %>
  <meta name="trace-identity" content="<%= identity %>">
<% end %>

<%# The event itself is a meta tag rather than a third <script>, for the other
    half of that Turbo rule: a meta tag is "provisional", so Turbo removes the
    old one and copies the new one on every visit, while a script would be
    appended and left there — one more dead tag in <head> for every page the
    student opens. lib/analytics.js reads this on turbo:load.

    Rendered whether or not the script above is — with no plausible on the page
    it is an inert tag nobody reads, and it means the events can be tested for
    without a spec having to pretend it is running in production. %>
<% if (event = tracked_event) %>
  <meta name="analytics-event" content="<%= event.to_json %>">
<% end %>
